          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Reflux symptoms are often worse after meals. The general clinical reference on GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease) lists large or late meals among the things that contribute to it. The same source notes that the pattern of symptoms, especially their link to meals and body position, is more informative than how severe they feel.\n\nThe lifestyle guide on eating with reflux gives two reasons:\n\n- **Fatty meals stay in the stomach longer.** They slow stomach emptying, which extends the time when reflux can happen. A heavy late dinner can cause symptoms that the same food at midday might not, because of how long the meal stays in the stomach.\n- **Some drinks and foods add to the problem.** Alcohol relaxes the valve between the stomach and food pipe and increases acid exposure. Carbonated drinks add gas that stretches the stomach.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not fully explain the underlying mechanism of why a full stomach pushes acid upward.\n\nThe clinical guideline on GERD notes that many people whose symptoms don't improve with acid-reducing treatment turn out to have a different cause. If your symptoms keep happening after meals or don't improve, discuss them with a clinician. The guideline excerpt I retrieved lists some warning signs, including a mass or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer, and new symptoms starting at an older age. The excerpt was incomplete, so it may not list every warning sign. My earlier version named trouble swallowing and weight loss as warning signs, but those did not appear in the retrieved text, so I have removed them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
